    "body": "This PR builds on top of #1951 (following the observation in https://github.com/bitcoin-core/secp256k1/pull/1951#issuecomment-5966873374). Only the last commit is new.\n\nIn `secp256k1_scalar_reduce_512`, all limb multiplications involve either `SECP256K1_N_C_0` (`0x402da1732fc9bebf`, ~0.251 * 2^64) or `SECP256K1_N_C_1` (`0x551231950b75fc44`, ~0.332 * 2^64). Because both constants have bit 63 cleared and their sum is ~0.583 * 2^64, the accumulator across both reduction phases peaks at `0x953fd3083b3fbb04ffffffffffffffff` (~0.583 * 2^128) under worst-case 512-bit inputs. It never overflows 128 bits, so the carry into `c2` is always zero.\n\nSwitching `reduce_512` to use `muladd_fast`, `sumadd_fast`, and `extract_fast` allows eliminating `c2` there entirely. The 128-bit macros (`init`, `muladd_fast`, `sumadd_fast`, `extract_fast`) are decoupled from `c2` to operate solely on `acc`, leaving `c2` strictly for the 192-bit accumulator in `mul_512`. The now-unused `sumadd` macro is also dropped.\n",
